One Pot Chicken Marsala Orzo
  --Alex Snodgrass

1 lb boneless, skinless chicken breasts, trimmed of excess fat, patted dry, and cut into 1" pieces
2 tsp kosher salt, divided
1/2 tsp freshly ground black pepper
1 tsp onion powder
1/2 tsp dried thyme
3 tbsp evoo, divided
1 tbsp unsalted butter
3 c stemmed and thinly sliced cremini mushrooms
1/2 c halved and thinly sliced shallot (~1 large shallot)
4 garlic cloves, minced
2 tsp fresh thyme leaves + more for serving
3/4 c marsala wine
1 c orzo (sub brown rice orzo or other small pasta for gluten-free)
2 c low-sodium chicken broth
Grated zest of 1/2 lemon
1 tbsp freshly squeezed lemon juice (about 1/2 lemon)
1/3 c crème fraiche

In a medium bowl, combine the chicken, 1 tsp of the salt, the pepper, onion powder, and dried thyme. Toss to evenly season the chicken. In a large, deep skillet or 3.5 qt braiser with fitted lid, heat 2 tbsp of the oil over medium-high heat. Add the chicken in a single layer and cook for 3-4 min per side until completely cooked through. Transfer to a plate and set aside. To the same skillet, add the remaining 1 tbsp oil and the butter. When the butter has melted, add the mushrooms and shallot. Cook, undisturbed, for 5 min to allow the mushrooms to get a good sear. Cook, stirring, for an additional 2-3 min, until the mushrooms are a deep golden brown. Reduce the heat to medium. Add the garlic, the remaining 1 tsp of salt, and the fresh thyme. Cook for 1 min, until the garlic is fragrant. Add the marsala and stir, scraping up any browned bits off the bottom of the pan. Cook until the marsala has reduced by half, ~1 min. Add the orzo, broth, and lemon zest. Stir to combine. Bring to a rapid simmer, then reduce heat to a gentle simmer, about medium-low heat, and cook for 8 min, stirring frequently, until most of the liquid has been absorbed and the orzo is almost done. Reduce the heat to low and add the chicken and any of its juices, the lemon juice, and the crème fraîche. Stir until well combined. Cover and cook until the chicken is warmed through and the orzo is al dente, 2-3 more min. 

